.TH SLABINFO 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
slabinfo \- report on and operate on the kernel's SLUB slab caches
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slabinfo
.RB [ \-aABDefilLnoPrsStTUvXz1 ]
.RB [ \-N
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-d [\c
.IR debug-flags ]]
.RI [ pattern ]
.br
.B slabinfo \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B slabinfo
reads the attributes of the slab caches of the SLUB allocator from
.I \%/sys/kernel/slab
and prints reports about them, or asks the kernel to shrink or validate
them.
.PP
Only caches whose names match
.I pattern
are processed.
.I pattern
is a POSIX basic regular expression, matched case-insensitively anywhere
in the name; the default matches every cache.
Caches that SLUB has merged appear in
.I \%/sys/kernel/slab
under generated names beginning with a colon, with symbolic links (the
aliases) for the names of the original caches; such caches are always
read, whatever the pattern.
In the list and the other per-cache reports, a merged cache with one
alias that matches
.I pattern
is shown under that alias name, a merged cache with several matching
aliases keeps its generated name unless
.B \-f
is given, and a merged cache with no matching alias is not shown.
.PP
When none of
.BR \-a ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-o ,
.BR \-r ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-t ,
.B \-v
and
.B \-d
is given,
.B slabinfo
lists the caches, as with
.BR \-l .
.B \-a
takes precedence over
.BR \-X ,
which takes precedence over
.BR \-T ;
otherwise one action is performed for each cache, the first of
.BR \-n ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-o ,
.B \-l
and
.B \-r
that is given.
.SS Cache list
The list printed by
.B \-l
has one line per cache with these columns:
.TP 16
.B Name
the cache name;
.TP
.B Objects
the number of objects allocated;
.TP
.B Objsize
the size of an object;
.TP
.B Space
the memory used by the cache's slabs, or, with
.BR \-L ,
.B Loss
instead: the part of that memory not used by objects;
.TP
.B Slabs/Part/Cpu
the number of slabs that are not CPU slabs, the number of partial slabs,
and the number of CPU slabs;
.TP
.B O/S
the number of objects per slab;
.TP
.B O
the page order of a slab;
.TP
.B %Fr
partial slabs as a percentage of all slabs;
.TP
.B %Ef
the memory used by objects as a percentage of the memory of the slabs;
.TP
.B Flg
flags:
.B *
the cache has aliases,
.B d
it uses DMA memory,
.B A
objects are aligned to hardware cache lines,
.B P
poisoning,
.B a
reclaim accounting,
.B Z
red zoning,
.B F
sanity checks,
.B U
user tracking,
.B T
tracing.
.PP
Sizes are shown in units scaled by powers of 1000, with the suffixes
.BR K ,
.B M
and
.BR G ,
unless
.B \-B
is given.
By default caches without slabs are not listed.
If
.I pattern
selects a single cache, its detailed report (see
.BR \-r )
is shown instead of a list line.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Reports
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-slabs
List the caches as described in
.IR "Cache list" .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-report
Print a detailed report for each cache: its sizes, slab counts, debug
options and memory use, its allocation and free traces (as with
.BR \-t ),
its per-node slab counts (as with
.BR \-n ),
and, when the kernel keeps them, its allocation statistics.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-aliases
Show the aliases of merged caches, as lines of the form
.IB cache " <- " "alias alias" \fR...,\fP
or, with
.BR \-i ,
as one line
.IB alias " -> " cache
per alias.
Aliases of caches that have only one alias are omitted unless
.B \-1
is given.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-numa
Print the number of slabs of each cache on each NUMA node.
On a system with a single node, a line saying that no NUMA information
is available is printed for each cache.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tracking
Print the allocation and free traces of each cache, read from
.IR \%/sys/kernel/debug/slab/ cache \%/alloc_traces
and
.IR \%free_traces ,
or
.B No Data
if they are not available.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-ops
Print the \%kmem_cache operations of each cache.
This kernel does not provide the attribute that this option reads, so
each cache is reported as having no \%kmem_cache operations.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-Totals
Print only summary information: the numbers of caches and aliases, the
total memory used and lost, and the average, minimum, maximum and total
of per-cache and per-object figures.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-Xtotals
Print the summary of
.BR \-T ,
followed by lists of the caches sorted by size, by loss and by number of
partial slabs, each limited to the number of caches set by
.B \-N
(default 1).
Implies
.BR \-B .
.SS List format and selection
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-display\-activity
Use a list format that shows activity: for each cache the number of
objects, the numbers of allocations and frees, the percentages of them
that took the fast path, the number of order fallbacks, the page order,
and the numbers of failed \%cmpxchg_double operations.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-Bytes
Show sizes in bytes.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-empty
List only the caches that have no slabs.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-zero
Also list the caches that have no slabs, and include them in the output
of
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-Unreclaim
List only caches that do not use reclaim accounting.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-first\-alias
Show a merged cache that has several aliases under one of its alias
names instead of its generated name.
.TP
.BR \-1 ", " \-\-1ref
With
.BR \-a ,
also show the aliases of caches that have a single alias.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-lines " \fIcount\fP"
Process only the first
.I count
caches in sort order (at least 1).
Caches that are processed but not printed, such as caches without slabs,
count towards the limit.
.SS Sorting
By default caches are sorted by name.
When more than one sort option is given, the first in the order
.BR \-S ,
.BR \-A ,
.BR \-L ,
.B \-P
applies.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-Size
Sort by the memory used, largest first.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-activity
Sort by activity (the total number of allocations and frees), most
active first.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-Loss
Sort by the memory not used by objects, largest first, and show it in the
list instead of the memory used.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-partial
Sort by the number of partial slabs, largest first.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-inverted
Reverse the sort order.
With
.BR \-a ,
print one line per alias, sorted by alias name.
.SS Operations
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-shrink
Ask the kernel to shrink each cache, by writing 1 to its
.I shrink
attribute.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-validate
Ask the kernel to validate the objects of each cache, by writing 1 to
its
.I validate
attribute.
The kernel reports problems it finds in the kernel log.
It validates only caches that have debugging enabled and rejects the
request for others;
.B slabinfo
does not report that rejection.
.TP
.BR \-d "[\fIdebug-flags\fP], " \-\-debug "[=\fIdebug-flags\fP]"
Request a change of the debug options of each cache, so that the options
in
.I debug-flags
are on and all others are off.
.I debug-flags
must be attached to the option.
It consists of the letters
.B f
(sanity checks),
.B z
(red zoning),
.B p
(poisoning),
.B u
(user tracking) and
.B t
(tracing), in either case, each at most once; the value
.B a
on its own stands for
.BR fzpu .
An empty value or
.B \-
turns every option off.
Sanity checks are turned on for any cache, but turning them off and
changing red zoning, poisoning or user tracking is attempted only for a
cache without objects, which
.B slabinfo
first shrinks if it still has slabs; for a cache with objects a message
is printed instead.
Tracing is turned on only if exactly one cache directory was read;
merged caches, which
.I pattern
does not filter, count too.
.IP
In this kernel the attributes that hold these options are read-only, so
any change that
.B slabinfo
attempts fails with the message
.RB \(dq "Cannot write to" \(dq
followed by the attribute, and
.B slabinfo
exits.
Debug options can be set at boot with the
.B \%slab_debug
kernel parameter, which is described in this file in the Linux source:
.RS
.I \%Documentation/admin\-guide/mm/slab.rst
.RE
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B slabinfo
exits with status 0 on success and with status 1 on error, including an
invalid option, an invalid pattern, an attribute that cannot be read for
lack of permission, and an attribute that cannot be written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%/sys/kernel/slab
The directory of slab caches; if it does not exist,
.I \%/sys/slab
is tried.
Each cache is a directory of attribute files, and each alias a symbolic
link to one.
.TP
.IR \%/sys/kernel/slab/ cache \%/shrink
.PD 0
.TP
.IR \%/sys/kernel/slab/ cache \%/validate
.PD
Written by
.B \-s
and
.BR \-v .
.TP
.IR \%/sys/kernel/debug/slab/ cache \%/alloc_traces
.PD 0
.TP
.IR \%/sys/kernel/debug/slab/ cache \%/free_traces
.PD
Read by
.BR \-t .
The kernel provides them in debugfs, which must be mounted on
.IR \%/sys/kernel/debug ,
and only for caches with user tracking enabled.
.SH NOTES
.B slabinfo
needs a kernel that uses SLUB with
.B \%CONFIG_SYSFS
support; otherwise it reports that SYSFS support for SLUB is not active.
The debug options and validation need
.BR \%CONFIG_SLUB_DEBUG ,
and the allocation statistics shown by
.BR \-r ,
.B \-A
and
.B \-D
need
.BR \%CONFIG_SLUB_STATS .
Several statistics that
.B slabinfo
reads, such as the slab deactivation counts in the report, are not
provided by this kernel and are shown as zero or omitted.
.PP
The attributes in
.I \%/sys/kernel/slab
are normally readable only by privileged users.
When an attribute cannot be read for lack of permission,
.B slabinfo
exits with the message
.RB \(dq "Try using superuser" \(dq.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the caches that use the most memory:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
slabinfo \-S \-N 10
.EE
.in
.PP
Show the detailed report for the dentry cache:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
slabinfo \-r dentry
.EE
.in
